": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-1322-0710"}, "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Department of Radiology, New York Presbyterian Hospital, Columbia University Medical Center, New York, NY, USA."}, {"affiliation": "Gustave Roussy, Université Paris-Saclay, Villejuif, France."}]}}, {"fullName": "Lu L", "lastName": "Lu", "firstName": "Lin", "initials": "L", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Department of Radiology, New York Presbyterian Hospital, Columbia University Medical Center, New York, NY, USA."}]}}, {"fullName": "Schwartz LH", "lastName": "Schwartz", "firstName": "Lawrence H", "initials": "LH", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Department of Radiology, New York Presbyterian Hospital, Columbia University Medical Center, New York, NY, USA."}]}}, {"fullName": "Qian M", "lastName": "Qian", "firstName": "Min", "initials": "M", "authorId": {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0003-1214-8830"}, "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Department of Biostatistics, Columbia University Medical Center, New York, NY, USA."}]}}, {"fullName": "Tejpar S", "lastName": "Tejpar", "firstName": "Sabine", "initials": "S", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Molecular Digestive Oncology, University Hospitals Leuven and KU Leuven, Leuven, Belgium."}]}}, {"fullName": "Eggleton P", "lastName": "Eggleton", "firstName": "Peter", "initials": "P", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Merck KGaA, Darmstadt, Germany."}]}}, {"fullName": "Zhao B", "lastName": "Zhao", "firstName": "Binsheng", "initials": "B", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Department of Radiology, New York Presbyterian Hospital, Columbia University Medical Center, New York, NY, USA."}]}}, {"fullName": "Piessevaux H", "lastName": "Piessevaux", "firstName": "Hubert", "initials": "H", "authorId": {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0003-1193-0601"}, "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Department of Hepato-Gastroenterology, Cliniques Universitaires Saint-Luc, UCLouvain Brussels, Brussels, Belgium."}]}}]}, "authorIdList": {"authorId": [{"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-1322-0710"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0003-1193-0601"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0003-1214-8830"}]}, "dataLinksTagsList": {"dataLinkstag": ["altmetrics", "supporting_data"]}, "journalInfo": {"issue": "9", "volume": "112", "journalIssueId": 3012645, "dateOfPublication": "2020 Sep", "monthOfPublication": 9, "yearOfPublication": 2020, "journal": {"medlineAbbreviation": "J Natl Cancer Inst", "title": "Journal of the National Cancer Institute", "essn": "1460-2105", "issn": "0027-8874", "nlmid": "7503089", "isoabbreviation": "J Natl Cancer Inst"}, "printPublicationDate": "2020-09-01"}, "pubYear": "2020", "pageInfo": "902-912", "abstractText": "<h4>Background</h4>The authors sought to forecast survival and enhance treatment decisions for patients with liver metastatic colorectal cancer by using on-treatment radiomics signature to predict tumor sensitiveness to irinotecan, 5-fluorouracil, and leucovorin (FOLFIRI) alone (F) or in combination with cetuximab (FC).<h4>Methods</h4>We retrospectively analyzed 667 metastatic colorectal cancer patients treated with F or FC. Computed tomography quality was classified as high (HQ) or standard (SD). Four datasets were created using the nomenclature (treatment) - (quality). Patients were randomly assigned (2:1) to training or validation sets: FCHQ: 78:38, FCSD: 124:62, FHQ: 78:51, FSD: 158:78. Four tumor-imaging biomarkers measured quantitative radiomics changes between standard of care computed tomography scans at baseline and 8 weeks. Using machine learning, the performance of the signature to classify tumors as treatment sensitive or treatment insensitive was trained and validated using receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curves. Hazard ratio and Cox regression models evaluated association with overall survival (OS).<h4>Results</h4>The signature (area under the ROC curve [95% confidence interval (CI)]) used temporal decrease in tumor spatial heterogeneity plus boundary infiltration to successfully predict sensitivity to antiepidermal growth factor receptor therapy (FCHQ: 0.80 [95% CI = 0.69 to 0.94], FCSD: 0.72 [95% CI = 0.59 to 0.83]) but failed with chemotherapy (FHQ: 0.59 [95% CI = 0.44 to 0.72], FSD: 0.55 [95% CI = 0.43 to 0.66]). In cetuximab-containing sets, radiomics signature outperformed existing biomarkers (KRAS-mutational status, and tumor shrinkage by RECIST 1.1) for detection of treatment sensitivity and was strongly associated with OS (two-sided P < .005).<h4>Conclusions</h4>Radiomics response signature can serve as an intermediate surrogate marker of OS. The signature outperformed known biomarkers in providing an early prediction of treatment sensitivity and could be used to guide cetuximab treatment continuation decisions.", "affiliation": "Department of Radiology, New York Presbyterian Hospital, Columbia University Medical Center, New York, NY, USA.", "publicationStatus": "ppublish", "language": "eng", "pubModel": "Print", "pubTypeList": {"pubType": ["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't", "research-article", "Validation Study", "Journal Article", "Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]}, "grantsList": {"grant": [{"agency": "NCI NIH HHS", "acronym": "CA", "grantId": "U01 CA225431", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "Fondation Philanthropia", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "National Cancer Institute", "grantId": "U01 CA140207", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "National Cancer Institute", "grantId": "U01 CA225431", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "Fondation Nuovo-Soldati", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NCI NIH HHS", "acronym": "CA", "grantId": "U01 CA140207", "orderIn": 0}]}, "meshHeadingList": {"meshHeading": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Humans"}, {"majorTopi